  "seoBody": "<div class=\"key-takeaways\"><strong>Key takeaways</strong><ul><li>Safe isolation and lockout tagout are part of the safe system of work required under the Health and Safety at Work etc Act 1974 and PUWER 1998</li><li>The steps are: identify every energy source, isolate, lock and tag, then verify a zero-energy state before work begins</li><li>Energy is not just electrical: it includes pneumatic, hydraulic, thermal, gravitational and stored energy such as springs and capacitors</li><li>LOTO usually sits inside a permit to work for higher-risk maintenance, so the audit confirms both the permit and the isolation</li><li>The audit does not isolate the machine: the lock, the tag and the verified zero-energy state do. The audit records that it was done</li></ul></div><h2>What a lockout tagout audit is</h2><p>Lockout tagout (LOTO) is the practice of isolating a machine's energy sources and locking them off before maintenance, so the machine cannot be started or release stored energy while someone is working on it. A LOTO audit is the check that this safe isolation was actually carried out, step by step, on a given job.</p><p>The audit does not isolate the machine. The lock, the tag and the verified zero-energy state do. What a good audit process does is make the isolation easy to record at the point of work, hard to skip a step, and fast to hand back to the office with photo evidence. Nobody should be signing off a safe isolation from memory after the maintenance is already done.</p><h2>Where LOTO fits in UK law</h2><p>There is no single regulation called lockout tagout in Great Britain. Safe isolation is part of the safe system of work an employer must provide under the Health and Safety at Work etc Act 1974, and the duty to make work equipment safe during maintenance under the Provision and Use of Work Equipment Regulations 1998 (PUWER). Electrical isolation also engages the Electricity at Work Regulations 1989. Treat this as general information and check the current regulations for your work.</p><h2>The safe isolation steps</h2><ul><li><strong>Identify:</strong> every energy source feeding the machine, electrical, pneumatic, hydraulic, thermal, gravitational and stored</li><li><strong>Isolate:</strong> shut down and isolate at the correct isolation point for each source</li><li><strong>Lock and tag:</strong> apply a lock and a personal danger tag, one lock per person working under the isolation where required</li><li><strong>Release stored energy:</strong> vent pressure, restrain springs, discharge stored electrical charge</li><li><strong>Verify:</strong> prove a zero-energy state, for example by attempting a start or testing for dead, before work begins</li></ul><p>The exact steps depend on the machine and the site's own isolation procedure. Treat a generic sequence as a starting point, not the whole procedure.</p><h2>LOTO and permit to work</h2><p>For higher-risk maintenance, lockout tagout usually sits inside a permit to work: a formal document that authorises the job, records the isolations, and hands control back only when the work is signed off and the locks are removed in a controlled order. The permit and the isolation belong together. A lock without an authorised permit, or a permit without a verified isolation, is a gap the audit is there to catch. Check your own permit-to-work procedure. This is not legal advice.</p><h2>What to look for in a lockout tagout audit tool</h2><ul><li><strong>Point-of-work recording:</strong> the isolation logged at the isolator, not signed off later at a desk</li><li><strong>Photo evidence:</strong> the applied lock and tag photographed at the point of observation</li><li><strong>Permit and isolation together:</strong> both captured in one workflow so neither is missed</li><li><strong>Only pay for who works:</strong> per-seat pricing punishes a site for its contractors. Quickler charges 20 pounds per active user a month, dormant users free, with a free tier of 20 reports a month</li><li><strong>Evidence trail:</strong> timestamp, engineer, machine, every isolation step, for the HSE inspection or insurer that surfaces years later</li></ul><div class=\"callout\"><strong>Honest note:</strong> Quickler records the safe isolation and the evidence and gives the plant a live dashboard. It does not isolate the machine, and it does not replace the competent person who authorises the permit or verifies the zero-energy state. It captures that the safe system of work was followed, with a timestamp and photo. <a href=\"/pages/help/index.html#contact-form-section\">Ask us</a> if that distinction matters for your site.</div><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>What is a lockout tagout (LOTO) audit?</h3><p>It is a check that a machine was safely isolated before maintenance: that every energy source was identified, isolated, locked and tagged, and that a zero-energy state was verified before work began. In Great Britain it is part of the safe system of work required under the Health and Safety at Work etc Act 1974 and PUWER 1998, rather than a single named regulation.</p><h3>What energy sources does a LOTO audit cover?</h3><p>All of them, not just electrical. A safe isolation audit covers electrical, pneumatic, hydraulic, thermal and gravitational energy, plus stored energy such as compressed springs, pressurised systems and charged capacitors. Each source has to be identified, isolated and, where relevant, released or restrained before the machine is considered safe.</p><h3>How does LOTO relate to a permit to work?</h3><p>For higher-risk maintenance, lockout tagout usually sits inside a permit to work. The permit authorises the job and records the isolations, and it hands control back only when the work is signed off and the locks are removed in a controlled order. The audit confirms both the permit and the isolation were in place. Check your own permit-to-work procedure and the current regulations. This is not legal advice.</p><h3>Can I run a safe isolation audit over WhatsApp?</h3><p>Yes. Quickler's lockout tagout workflow runs over the WhatsApp Business API. The engineer receives each step in their existing WhatsApp chat, replies with text, a voice note or a photo of the applied lock, and the completed audit generates automatically. No separate app or login is required, and quickler manages the WhatsApp Business API account on the firm's behalf.</p><h3>Related guides</h3><ul><li><a href=\"/pages/factory-audit-software-uk.html\">Factory audit software</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/machine-safety-inspection-checklist-uk.html\">Machine safety inspection checklist (PUWER)</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/5s-workplace-audit-app-uk.html\">5S workplace audit app</a></li></ul>",
